WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.640
The biggest superhero team-up of all time
— for now — has finally dropped.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.720
Avengers: Infinity War is the payoff of ten
years of storytelling done over the course

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.230
of nearly 20 films.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:15.059
The movie follows the Avengers, the Guardians
of the Galaxy, and a smattering of other superheroes

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:19.650
as they take on the Mad Titan, Thanos, who
seeks to assemble the Infinity Gauntlet with

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.529
the intent of wiping out half of all life
in existence.

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:26.650
That obviously doesn't bode well for, uh,
existence — so heroes from all across the

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.500
Marvel Cinematic Universe have to step up
and do everything they can to stop him.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.180
The film is a funny, action-packed, and ultimately
bittersweet romp that somehow pulls off the

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.080
ambitious balancing act it sets out to accomplish.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.180
By the time it ends, our heroes and the universe
they inhabit have been forever changed.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.739
It isn't perfect, but it's pretty damn near
close.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:49.000
Obviously, watch out for some heavy duty spoilers,
because we're going to ruin this movie's plot

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.629
twists like Thanos ruined — well, you'll
see.

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:56.980
These are the five best and five worst things
about Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.190
Best: Hulk meets his match

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.920
You've got to start a movie like Infinity
War off with a bang — and this one starts

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.610
off with one heck of a wallop.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.000
We open on the aftermath of Thanos' assault
on the Asgardian refugee ship, with bodies

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.930
littering the floor — but the tides seem
to turn when Loki informs Thanos that his

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.140
team has a Hulk.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.600
Longtime fans obviously caught this line as
an echo of this famous exchange:

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.930
"I have an army."

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.340
"We have a Hulk!"

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.330
For once, Loki is on the side of the big green
guy — not that it ends up helping this time

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:29.159
around.The slugfest of the century follows,
albeit one that doesn't quite go the way you'd

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:30.159
think.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.920
Even ambushing Thanos from a blind spot, the
big green dude meets his match with the Mad

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:34.920
Titan.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:45.439
We've seen Hulk wreck every hero in the MCU,
but in this fight it becomes clear that it's

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:47.939
only because nobody can match his strength.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.500
Because he has that to fall back on, he's
never had to actually learn to fight — just

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.500
smash.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.420
With the help of the purple power stone, Thanos
is just as strong as Hulk, with the added

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.759
bonus of his own actual combat skills.

00:01:58.759 --> 00:02:03.500
He dismantles Hulk quickly, and it's a genuinely
shocking moment, setting the tone for bigger,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.619
even more depressing surprises to come.

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:08.030
Worst: A bad perspective

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.760
Game of Thrones actor Peter Dinklage appears
in Infinity War as Eitri, the giant dwarf

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.510
who forged Thor's hammer.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.430
Marvel has a pretty good track record with
CGI transformations, be it shrinking Chris

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:24.020
Evans down to 90 pounds or turning Mark Ruffalo's
motion-captured Hulk performance into a believable

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.220
CGI character.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:30.069
Unfortunately, Dinklage's character doesn't
quite get that sort of detail-oriented treatment.

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:31.599
It all comes down to perspective.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.340
In order for an audience to properly gauge
size differences between characters, we need

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.370
to see them standing in the frame with one
another.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.770
It allows for the eyes to assess the proportions
of the characters onscreen, and it's especially

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.319
crucial when dealing with characters who are
intentionally enlarged or shrunk.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:50.060
Unfortunately, while we can see that Dinklage's
character is a giant from looking at his surroundings,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.330
we don't see him standing next to Rocket or
Thor nearly long enough to fully understand

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.330
his size.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:59.650
As a result, he feels kind of isolated, and
there's no perspective for the character's

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:00.650
build.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.980
The eye can't help but notice something is
off about him because of the lack of perspective.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.019
Lord of the Rings this is not.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:08.300
Best: The brightest light

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.909
Chris Hemsworth is a standout performer in
the MCU, and Infinity War might be a better

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:15.640
spotlight for him than any of his solo movies.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.569
Thor is easily the standout character of Infinity
War, with a winning performance from Hemsworth

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:22.830
and an odd-couple pairing with Rocket Raccoon.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.930
The two head off into space with Teenage Groot
in order to find a new weapon for Thor.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.780
Their pairing is funny and heartfelt, but
rather than being played for nonstop laughs,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.920
it pulls back and reminds viewers just how
much Thor has lost over the course of these

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.920
movies.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.630
While the character of Thor has only gotten
funnier, his life has gotten so much worse

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.000
at the same time.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.379
Everyone he loves is dead, and he feels much
of the blame.

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:47.240
The Rocket we met in Guardians of the Galaxy
would have joked about this, but this more

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.790
mature Rocket is actually a nice companion.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.569
But nothing compares to Thor's triumphant
return to Earth with his new weapon, Stormbreaker.

00:03:54.569 --> 00:03:58.380
Even without the Led Zeppelin soundtrack from
his last movie, the god of thunder has never

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.410
looked more godly.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.110
Worst: A fatal fit of mischief

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.640
There was a time when you could argue that
Loki was the most dangerous character in the

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.080
entire MCU, but the frequent co-star and occasional
villain ultimately becomes more of a pest

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.439
than a major threat.

00:04:12.439 --> 00:04:17.230
Nonetheless, Loki's arc has still been a compelling
one, despite getting something of an unceremonious

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:18.739
ending in Infinity War.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:23.280
The God of Mischief bites the dust in the
film's opening scene after attempting to double-cross

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.280
Thanos.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.310
It's quick and it's dirty, with his demise
ultimately just serving to fuel Thor's arc

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.310
in the film.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.770
It's not the most satisfying conclusion to
the story of one of the MCU's most compelling

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:34.770
characters.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.430
If Loki really is a goner, we can't help but
wish he'd gone out a little differently.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.040
Best: Anti-hero

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.020
Villains have never been the MCU's strong
suit, but with this movie, they not only made

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.300
a great one — they actually made him the
film's protagonist.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.560
Obviously Thanos isn't the film's "good guy"
or anything, but Infinity War's story is structured

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.330
entirely around him and his quest.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.300
He's the character we learn the most about,
and the one whose story most closely sticks

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.090
to traditional three-act structure.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:03.470
We see him struggle, make difficult choices,
and then move forward with the ramifications

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.690
of those choices.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:08.720
It's Thanos we see in the film's closing shot,
looking out on a universe he now believes

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.850
to be at peace.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:13.920
And obviously everyone loves a good Antihero
when he's this good at being bad.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.660
"We're just getting started.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.340
Nothing stops this train, nothing."

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.020
Worst: They'll be back

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.720
The same actors can't play these characters
forever, so we all knew some bad things were

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.810
coming in this team-up.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.910
When the film opens with Loki and Heimdall
getting taken out by Thanos, it sets a precedent.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:38.430
From there, the movie doesn't let up, as hero
after hero gets battered, bruised, and beaten.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.789
But while the film's devastating ending features
half of the life in the universe crumbling

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:45.729
into dust, it doesn't quite hold the weight
it should.

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:49.639
Let's be real for a second: does anybody actually
think all of these characters are gone for

00:05:49.639 --> 00:05:50.639
good?

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:51.639
Of course not.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.850
That's the tricky thing about erasing the
bulk of your cast from existence — most

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.759
of the audience can probably call your bluff.

00:05:56.759 --> 00:06:01.160
Gamora, Loki, and Heimdall's deaths feel more
permanent right now because all these Marvel

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.060
movies can theoretically continue on without
them, even with Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.539
3 having already been announced.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:10.889
But once the rest of the cast starts to drop
— namely all of the members of the next

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:14.979
incarnation of the Avengers — it resonates
more in the moment due to how shocking it

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:15.979
is.

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:19.650
The ultimate goal of this move is likely to
focus Avengers 4 on the original core cast,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:20.650
all of whom survived.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:24.759
As much as we hate to admit it, knowing that
kind of makes the film's shocking finale feel

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:27.140
a little less weighty after some thought.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.030
Best: Let it breathe

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:33.410
Marvel has had its comedic tone on lock since
day one with Tony Stark's quips in Iron Man

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.120
— but there is such a thing as too much
of a good thing.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.630
Marvel's movies have been known to occasionally
lean into the laughs at the wrong moments,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.120
using humor to stifle emotional beats rather
than let them play out.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:47.700
"Ow!

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:48.530
My Nipples!

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.620
Agghhhhh!"

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.990
Fortunately, that doesn't happen so much here.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:56.979
There's plenty of humor throughout the film,
but rarely does it come at an inappropriate

00:06:56.979 --> 00:06:57.979
time.

00:06:57.979 --> 00:07:00.210
Thor's therapy session with Rocket goes uninterrupted.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.240
Star-Lord's realization that Gamora is dead
plays out to its fullest extent.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.179
Most importantly, the tragic ending is never
interrupted with a goofy quip.

00:07:08.179 --> 00:07:12.240
This is probably the single most important
film in the MCU, and the fact that they managed

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.349
to balance the tone perfectly in it makes
for a much more satisfying viewing experience.

00:07:17.349 --> 00:07:19.479
Worst: Cap it off

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:23.340
With dozens of different characters and plot
threads to balance, it's only natural that

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:27.030
some characters are going to get more screentime
and character development than others.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.389
Nonetheless, there's a weird bit of disparity
in this regard when it comes to Captain America

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:34.419
and his crew — Black Widow, Falcon, and
Winter Soldier.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:38.740
Cap's Secret Avengers are onscreen for a very
solid chunk of the film's running time, but

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:43.000
it's strange how empty their time feels compared
to the Thor and Iron Man parties, who get

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.680
plenty to do emotionally.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.620
It's not for a lack of material, either.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:50.880
Steve Rogers is in a very difficult, very
interesting spot in the wake of Captain America:

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.000
Civil War, lacking his signature shield and
his stars-and-stripes identity.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.889
But in Infinity War, Cap and his gang are
pretty much just there to throw punches.

00:07:58.889 --> 00:08:00.319
When they do, it's awesome.

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:04.830
But we seriously wish Team Cap got a bit more
of an engaging, emotional arc.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.430
Best: Sticking the landing

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:11.360
Balancing ten years, 19 movies, and dozens
of characters and plot threads makes Infinity

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.120
War without exaggeration one of the most ambitious
blockbusters of all time.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.300
There's so much going against the movie that
it shouldn't work.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.390
Amazingly, it does.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:22.009
The film as a whole is a triumph in spite
of itself.

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:23.650
It lacks traditional structure.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:27.650
There's very little time for real character
development - unless you count getting cosmically

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.509
turned to ash as character development.

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:34.640
It also relies entirely on the viewer having
seen all 18 movies leading up to it, yet somehow

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.890
it never feels hindered by its ambitions.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:41.149
It knows that what viewers want is a big,
bombastic rollercoaster ride, and it delivers

00:08:41.149 --> 00:08:45.130
- all while giving almost everyone in its
cast a great moment or two.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.240
Avengers: Infinity War should crumble under
the weight of its own ambitions.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.320
That it stands tall instead and makes almost
three hours fly by is a… marvelous accomplishment.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.500
"Alright, hey!"

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:56.580
"Alright, good job, guys."

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.180
"Let's just not come in tomorrow.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:01.340
Let's just…take a day."

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.900
"You ever try shawarma?"

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.300
Worst: Accent amnesia

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.770
Marvel Studios' movies are consistent.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:11.589
They're consistently good, they're consistent
in tone, they're consistent in quality of

00:09:11.589 --> 00:09:13.260
casting decisions and performances.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.170
But you know what isn't consistent at all
in Avengers: Infinity War?

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:18.950
Scarlet Witch's accent.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:23.250
Throughout her debut turn in Avengers: Age
of Ultron, Wanda speaks with a thick accent.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.680
She mostly keeps it throughout Civil War,
too.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.360
But Elizabeth Olsen seems to have had trouble
committing to Wanda's accent in Infinity War.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.910
One scene it's there; the next, she sounds
like, well, Elizabeth Olsen.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.350
Of course, exactly nobody would particularly
care if she just got rid of the accent altogether.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.290
But constantly switching in and out of it
— sometimes multiple times in the same scene

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.900
— just makes it stand out.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.630
Fortunately, in a movie featuring a talking
raccoon, magic stones, and a giant purple

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:53.000
warlord, it's easy enough to just ignore Scarlet
Witch's magical, disappearing accent.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Thanks for watching!

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.760
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.079
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

